  <!-- FORMULA -->
  <section class="roi-section" id="formula">
    <h2>ROI Formula Explained in Plain English</h2>
    <p>At its core, ROI answers one question: <em>how much did you make relative to what you spent?</em> It's the single most important metric for any investor, business owner, or marketer in the USA.</p>
    <div class="roi-formula">
      <code><span>ROI</span> = (Net Profit / Total Investment) × 100</code>
      <code><span>Net Profit</span> = Total Return − Total Investment</code>
      <code>Example: Invest $10,000 → Receive $13,000 → Net Profit $3,000</code>
      <code><span>ROI</span> = ($3,000 / $10,000) × 100 = <span>30%</span></code>
    </div>

    <h3>Annualized ROI (CAGR)</h3>
    <p>Simple ROI doesn't account for how long your money was tied up. A 30% return over 3 years is very different from 30% in 6 months. Annualized ROI (also called CAGR — Compound Annual Growth Rate) puts all investments on a level playing field.</p>
    <div class="roi-formula">
      <code><span>Annualized ROI</span> = ((1 + ROI)^(1/Years) − 1) × 100</code>
      <code>Example: 30% ROI over 3 years → Annualized ROI = <span>9.14%/yr</span></code>
    </div>

    <h3>After-Tax ROI</h3>
    <p>Always calculate after-tax ROI for a realistic picture — because the IRS always gets its share. Capital gains tax, ordinary income tax, depreciation recapture, and state income taxes all reduce your actual return. In states like Texas and Florida (0% state income tax), after-tax ROI is naturally higher than in California (up to 13.3%) or New York (up to 10.9%).</p>
    <div class="roi-formula">
      <code><span>After-Tax Net Profit</span> = Net Profit × (1 − Tax Rate)</code>
      <code><span>After-Tax ROI</span> = (After-Tax Net Profit / Investment) × 100</code>
    </div>

    <h3>How to Calculate ROI in Excel</h3>
    <p>Many investors prefer tracking ROI in spreadsheets. Here's the simplest Excel formula for return on investment:</p>
    <div class="roi-formula">
      <code>Cell A1: Net Profit (e.g., 3000)</code>
      <code>Cell B1: Total Investment (e.g., 10000)</code>
      <code>Cell C1: <span>=A1/B1*100</span> → Result: <span>30%</span></code>
      <code>Annualized: <span>=((1+(A1/B1))^(1/3)-1)*100</span> → <span>9.14%</span></code>
    </div>
    <p>For a more robust setup, <a href="#download">download our free Excel ROI template</a> with pre-built formulas for all investment types, or read our complete step-by-step tutorial on <a href="/blog/how-to-calculate-roi" style="font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;">how to calculate ROI across real estate, marketing, and Amazon FBA</a>.</p>

    <div class="roi-info">💡 <strong>Payback Period</strong> = Total Investment / Annual Net Profit. A $10,000 investment generating $3,000/year pays back in 3.33 years. The shorter the payback, the lower your risk exposure.</div>
  </section>

