  <!-- MISTAKES -->
  <section class="roi-section" id="mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating ROI</h2>
    <p>Even experienced investors and business owners make these errors. Avoid them to get accurate, reliable ROI figures:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ignoring all costs.</strong> Only counting the purchase price and forgetting closing costs, repairs, holding costs, and selling costs can make ROI look 30–50% better than reality. Always use <em>total</em> investment, not just the sticker price.</li>
      <li><strong>Using gross rent, not net income.</strong> A 10% gross rental yield in Michigan often drops to 5–6% net after vacancy, maintenance, property management, insurance, and taxes. Always use net operating income for real estate ROI calculations.</li>
      <li><strong>Skipping taxes.</strong> Capital gains tax, income tax on rental income, depreciation recapture, and state-level taxes all eat into returns. A 30% ROI at a 25% combined tax rate drops to a 22.5% after-tax ROI. This is why our calculator includes a tax rate input.</li>
      <li><strong>Not annualizing.</strong> A 50% ROI over 5 years sounds great but is only 8.4% annualized — potentially below the S&P 500 average. Always compare investments on an annualized basis to make fair comparisons.</li>
      <li><strong>Confusing ROI with cash-on-cash return.</strong> ROI measures total return on total investment. Cash-on-cash return measures annual cash flow against cash invested (your down payment). Both metrics matter for leveraged real estate deals — use our <a href="/real-estate/cash-on-cash-return-calculator">cash-on-cash calculator</a> for the latter.</li>
      <li><strong>Ignoring the time value of money.</strong> For projects lasting more than 2–3 years, use Net Present Value (NPV) or Internal Rate of Return (IRR). $25,000 received in Year 5 is worth significantly less than $25,000 today at any reasonable discount rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Attributing all revenue to one campaign.</strong> Marketing ROI is only accurate when attribution is correct. Use UTM parameters, promo codes, or multi-touch attribution models to avoid inflated figures. This is especially important for SEO ROI calculations where organic traffic compounds over time.</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting opportunity cost.</strong> Money invested in a 5% ROI asset is money not invested elsewhere. Always compare against your best alternative use of capital — for most US investors, that benchmark is the S&P 500's historical 7–10% average annual return.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="roi-warn">⚠️ <strong>Amazon FBA sellers:</strong> A common error is calculating ROI on product cost only, forgetting Amazon's FBA fulfillment fee (avg $3–$6/unit), referral fee (8–15%), monthly storage fee, PPC advertising spend, prep costs, and return processing costs. Always calculate on total landed cost. Use the Amazon FBA tab above or our dedicated <a href="/business/amazon-roi-calculator" style="color:#92400e;text-decoration:underline;">Amazon ROI calculator</a> for accurate results.</div>
    <div class="roi-warn">⚠️ <strong>Solar investors:</strong> Don't forget to factor in the 30% federal Investment Tax Credit (ITC) when calculating net investment. Also account for annual electricity price increases (averaging 3% nationally), which <em>increase</em> your savings over time and significantly improve long-term ROI.</div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="roi-section" id="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <% const _faqs = [["How do you calculate ROI?", "ROI = (Net Profit / Total Investment) × 100. Net profit equals your total return minus your total investment cost. Example: invest $10,000, receive $13,000 back → net profit = $3,000 → ROI = 30%. For multi-year investments, also calculate annualized ROI using the CAGR formula: ((1 + ROI)^(1/Years) − 1) × 100. Use the calculator above for instant, accurate results."], ["How is ROI calculated for rental property?", "Rental property ROI = (Annual Net Cash Flow / Total Cash Invested) × 100. Annual net cash flow = gross annual rent minus vacancy (typically 5–8%), property taxes, insurance, maintenance (budget 1% of property value annually), property management fees (8–10%), and mortgage principal &amp; interest payments. Total cash invested = down payment + closing costs + upfront repairs. In strong rental markets like Ohio, Michigan, and Georgia, 8–12% cash-on-cash returns are achievable."], ["How do you calculate ROI in Excel?", "In Excel: enter your net profit in A1, total investment in B1, then use the formula =A1/B1*100 in C1. For annualized ROI: =((1+(A1/B1))^(1/years)-1)*100. For rental property ROI: create columns for monthly income, expenses, and cash flow, then sum annually and divide by total cash invested. <a href=\"#download\">Download our free Excel ROI template</a> with pre-built formulas for all investment types."], ["How do you calculate marketing ROI?", "Marketing ROI = ((Revenue Attributed to Campaign − Campaign Cost) / Campaign Cost) × 100. Example: a $5,000 Google Ads campaign generates $18,000 in tracked revenue → Marketing ROI = ($13,000 / $5,000) × 100 = 260%. The industry benchmark is a 5:1 ROAS minimum ($5 revenue per $1 spent). Always use UTM tracking, Google Analytics 4 attribution, or CRM data to ensure accurate measurement. Try our <a href=\"/business/marketing-roi-calculator\">marketing ROI calculator</a> for detailed analysis."], ["How do you calculate ROI in real estate?", "Real estate ROI has two components: (1) Cash-on-cash ROI = Annual Cash Flow / Cash Invested × 100 — this measures your annual return on actual cash deployed. (2) Total ROI includes property appreciation, equity buildup through mortgage paydown, and any tax benefits like depreciation. For a $250,000 property with 20% down ($50,000 + $5,000 closing costs), $2,400/yr net cash flow, and 3.5% annual appreciation over 5 years, total ROI can reach 60–80%. States like Texas and Florida (no state income tax) produce higher after-tax ROI."], ["How do you calculate ROI for a project or IT investment?", "Project ROI = ((Total Benefits − Total Costs) / Total Costs) × 100. Benefits include revenue generated, cost savings, productivity improvements, and risk reduction. For large enterprise projects, use Net Present Value (NPV) to discount future benefits by your cost of capital (typically 8–12% for US businesses). Example: a $50,000 automation project generating $25,000/year in savings over 3 years has a simple ROI of 50%, but the NPV-adjusted ROI accounts for the time value of those future savings."], ["How do you calculate Amazon FBA ROI?", "Amazon FBA ROI = ((Revenue − Total Costs) / Total Costs) × 100. Total costs must include: product cost (sourcing + manufacturing), Amazon referral fee (8–15%), FBA fulfillment fee (avg $3–$6/unit), monthly storage fees, PPC advertising spend, prep/labeling, and return processing costs. Target 30%+ ROI per product to build a sustainable business. Top Amazon FBA sellers achieve 50–100%+ ROI per product. Use our <a href=\"/business/amazon-roi-calculator\">Amazon ROI calculator</a> for a detailed breakdown."], ["How do you calculate digital marketing ROI and influencer ROI?", "Digital marketing ROI = ((Attributed Revenue − Marketing Spend) / Marketing Spend) × 100. Track revenue using UTM parameters, Google Analytics, or your CRM. Industry average digital marketing ROI is 5:1 ($5 earned per $1 spent). For influencer campaigns: Influencer ROI = ((Revenue from Promo Code − Influencer Fee) / Influencer Fee) × 100. Also measure Earned Media Value (EMV) = total impressions × industry CPM to capture brand awareness value beyond direct sales."], ["What is a good ROI in 2026?", "Benchmarks vary by asset class in the USA: S&amp;P 500 (historical average): 7–10% annually. Rental real estate: 8–12% cash-on-cash in strong markets (Ohio, Michigan, Georgia). Marketing campaigns: 5:1 ROAS minimum. Solar panels: 10–20% annualized after the 30% ITC. Amazon FBA: 30–100%+ per product. Business automation: 100–400% over 3 years. A \"good\" ROI always depends on the risk level — higher risk should demand proportionally higher returns."], ["What is the difference between ROI, IRR, and CAGR?", "ROI is a simple percentage of total profit vs total cost — it doesn't account for time. CAGR (Compound Annual Growth Rate) annualizes the ROI to show a per-year return, making it easy to compare investments of different durations. IRR (Internal Rate of Return) is the annualized rate at which the Net Present Value of all cash flows equals zero — it accounts for the timing and size of each individual cash flow. For simple investments, use ROI or CAGR. For complex multi-year projects with uneven cash flows (common in commercial real estate and software projects), IRR is more accurate."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %>
    <div class="roi-faq-item">
      <div class="roi-faq-q"><%- f[0] %></div>
      <div class="roi-faq-a"><%- f[1] %></div>
    </div>
    <% }); %>
  </section>

